Title:
Shelter (ROP-based Sleep Obfuscation for In-Memory Payload Encryption)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Shelter is a Rust crate implementing a “weaponized” sleep-obfuscation technique that encrypts an in-memory payload (optionally the entire mapped PE) while the thread is sleeping.  
- It aims to reduce exposure to memory scanners by AES-128 encrypting code/data pages and removing execute permissions during the sleep window, then restoring protections on wake.  
- The implementation avoids common sleep-obfuscation primitives (APCs, hardware breakpoints, timers) and instead uses ROP chains, combined with call stack spoofing via the author’s Unwinder project.  
- It supports multiple ways to locate the PE base (standard `MZ`, explicit base address, or a custom 2-byte “magic” pattern to allow header removal).  
- OPSEC-oriented choices are highlighted (DInvoke_rs, indirect syscalls, string literal encryption), and testing notes discuss PE-sieve behavior including high-entropy detection when fully encrypted.  
- Useful primarily for red teams, implant developers, and offensive security researchers evaluating memory-evasion tradeoffs and detection artifacts.

Technical Focus:
- ROP-chain based sleep obfuscation (no APC/HWBP/timers)
- In-memory PE/section encryption (AES-128) and protection flipping (RX→RW)
- Call stack spoofing / unwinding manipulation (Unwinder)
- PE base discovery techniques (MZ scan, explicit base, custom magic bytes)
- Native sleep via `NtWaitForSingleObject` + optional event signaling
- OPSEC tradeoffs vs memory scanners (PE-sieve, entropy-based heuristics)

Use Cases:
- Integrate sleep-time in-memory encryption into Rust implants/loaders
- Encrypt entire manually-mapped DLL/PE during idle periods to reduce static indicators in memory
- Combine header removal/custom magic bytes with self-obfuscation for stealthier manual mapping
- Research/demonstrate ROP-only obfuscation flows and stack spoofing impacts on tooling
- Evaluate detection surface: executable-page scans vs data-page scans vs entropy heuristics

Keywords:
Rust, sleep obfuscation, ROP, return-oriented programming, in-memory encryption, AES-128, PE encryption, manual mapping, NtWaitForSingleObject, SetEvent, memory protections, VirtualProtect, call stack spoofing, Unwinder, DInvoke_rs, indirect syscalls, PE-sieve, high entropy detection, MZ header removal, OPSEC